  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 323 AM EDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis and Forecast: Split flow midlevel pattern begins to \ndevelop across North America today with shallow ridging over the \nU.S/Canadian Border and disheveled troughing across the \nCentral/Southern CONUS. With the aforementioned midlevel ridging \nregime across the Northern U.S expected to last through the next \nseveral days, high pressure will build at the surface and support \nrain-free weather through the entirety of the forecast period. Post \nfrontal northeast flow will result in today being the coolest day of \nthe week with highs in the upper 50s east of I-75 while southwest \nparts of the CWA remain water due to slight downsloping processes. \nFocus remains on tonight as clearing cloud cover and efficient \nradiational cooling processes will lead to chilly overnight lows. \nInterior Eastern Upper will very likely see frost development in the \nmorning while interior northern lower remains in the upper 30s. \nPatchy frost is still expected to develop, but will remain more \nlocalized compared to eastern upper. \n\nTemperatures will remain near normal for mid to late September\nthrough the remainder of the week with highs in the 60s for \nmost locations. The continued high pressure across the Great \nLakes Region will keep partly to mostly sunny skies and no \nchances of precipitation through the next several days. Long \nrange ensembles depict an embedded shortwave supporting some \nshower development this weekend in the northern plains. \nConfidence with this feature remains low as the midlevel wave \nappears to be lifting/dissipating with time causing the boundary\nto likely fall apart before reaching Michigan combined with the\nsystem remaining very moisture starved. Low PoPs will remain in\nthe forecast at this time, but will likely leave the long term \nin the next few forecast cycles.",
